# Sensitive Data Guidelines

### Sensitive Data Rules

#### When to Check These Rules
- Before committing changes
- When handling credentials
- When working with API keys
- When dealing with user data
- When configuring environments

#### Rules
##### What to Check For
- API keys and secrets
- Passwords and credentials
- Personal identifiable information
- Financial data
- Environment variables
- Configuration files

##### Best Practices
- Use environment variables
- Never commit .env files
- Use secure credential storage
- Follow data encryption standards
- Implement proper access controls
- Log sensitive data access
- Use proper key rotation
- Implement audit logging
- Follow least privilege principle

#### Examples
✅ Correct:
```ruby
# Using environment variables
ENV['API_KEY']
Rails.application.credentials.api_key

# Secure credential storage
Rails.application.credentials.aws[:access_key_id]
```

❌ Incorrect:
- Hardcoded API keys
- Committed .env files
- Plain text passwords
- Unencrypted sensitive data
- Broad access permissions

#### Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Hardcoding sensitive data
- Committing credentials
- Not using environment variables
- Poor access control
- No audit logging
- Not rotating keys
- Not encrypting data

#### Validation Checklist
- [ ] No hardcoded credentials
- [ ] Environment variables used
- [ ] No .env files committed
- [ ] Proper encryption used
- [ ] Access controls in place
- [ ] Audit logging implemented
- [ ] Key rotation planned
- [ ] Least privilege followed
